  grid: 20-30 rows x 20-30 cols, square [observed]
  background: single dominant color
  control_row: row 0 is all background except 2-6 control pixels at distinct columns
  control_pixels:
    count: 2-6, each a non-bg color; duplicate colors allowed (same color at different cols)
    spacing: gaps between consecutive control columns >= 3
  rectangles:
    count: 1-6 solid axis-aligned rectangles placed in rows 1..H-1
    size: 5-14 rows x 6-19 cols each
    colors: each rect has a single non-bg color; rect colors may or may not match a control color
    overlap: rectangles may overlap; later-placed rect's color wins in the overlap region
    edge_touch: rects may touch grid edges (left/right/bottom) but not row 0
  ray_interaction: each control column must pass through at least one rectangle
  branch_coverage: REQUIRED; every input grid contains both ray branches:
    at least one control ray hits a cell of its own color (toggle off to bg),
    and at least one control ray hits a non-bg cell of a different color
    (overwrite to the control color)
  transform:
    for each control pixel at (0, c) with color K, process column c in rows 1..H-1
    cells equal to K become background (toggle off); all other cells become K (toggle on)
    non-control columns and row 0 are unchanged; output grid same size as input

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size (square, 20-30). Choose background color.
    2. Choose N control columns (2-6) with gap >= 3. Assign each a non-bg color.
    3. Place 1-6 solid rectangles in rows 1..H-1, painting in z-order (later rects overwrite).
       Ensure each control column intersects at least one rectangle's bounding box.
       Ensure the final painted grid has at least one same-color ray hit and
       at least one different-foreground-color ray hit across the controls.
    4. This gives the input grid. The output is computed by the toggle rule.
       No inversion needed since forward transform is trivial to apply.
